Negative control for scenario-player.spec.ts (T4.2, AC2.6 — design §3.1 ruling 1
/ §7.2, the ARM ORDER).

A scene is an imperative call on a live composable, and `remove`, `ensure` and
`setDefault` are among the client-emails page's own — so `arm` arms the `replay` preset
BEFORE the first scene runs. This mutant moves that arm to after every scene,
which means the first scene of any track (its arrangement — the one that boots
the cell and fetches the collection) leaves the app for the module's endpoints,
and the first scrub, which replays from scene 0, re-fires the WRITING scenes
against staging. That is the defect design §7.2 names verbatim: "if the arming
order ever slips, a scrub writes to staging".

The mutant is deliberately surgical — the same call, one step later. Every other
number the transport publishes (playhead, status, scene order) is untouched, so
a spec that only counts scenes stays green: the zero-request claim is the one
thing that goes.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: scenario-player.spec.ts — the AC2.6 claims (the `replay` preset is armed
before the first scene's call, and zero requests leave the app for the module's
endpoints while a track plays).

